
  Last question before I put approaches together. Greenfield means I pick a stack, and that's not a detail I should
  choose silently — it determines everything about how the code is written and what you'll be maintaining.

  - [1mNode + TypeScript[0m (Express or Fastify, Postgres, plain frontend or React) — one language across client and server,
    the largest ecosystem for this shape of app, and the notification/subscription patterns are extremely well-trodden
    here. Heaviest node_modules footprint.
  - [1mPython[0m (FastAPI, Postgres, SQLAlchemy) — best if you or your team already live in Python. Strong typing story with
    Pydantic, excellent testing. Means two languages, since the frontend is still JS.
  - [1mGo[0m (net/http or chi, Postgres) — single binary, trivial deploy, great for the long-lived connections you'd want if
    real-time is ever added. Smaller ecosystem for app scaffolding; more code written by hand.

  Also cheap now and expensive later: [1mPostgres vs SQLite.[0m SQLite is genuinely fine for a multi-user task app at small
  scale and removes a whole service from the deploy. Postgres is the safer default if this is ever more than a handful
  of people.

  [1mMy recommendation: Node + TypeScript with Postgres[0m, unless your team has a language preference — in which case that
  preference wins outright, because the best stack here is the one whoever maintains it already knows.
